{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And the priest shall burn the whole on the altar, a burnt-offering, an offering made by fire, a sweet savor unto YHWH.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the purpose of the sacrifices is all for the benefit of man, for the will of HaShem, exalted be He, is in man, who is the chosen of the species and for whose sake the world was created — that he be entirely intellectual, like the angel of YHWH of hosts, without sin. And when he sins, bearing guilt from the side of the evil impulse planted in him, it is fitting that he repent and recognize and reflect upon the lowliness of himself and the measure of the exaltation of the Master, exalted be He, whose mouth he disobeyed, and he is obligated to set it upon his heart that he has sinned before Him with his body and with his soul. And since all a person's actions are comprised in three things, namely deed, speech, and thought — these being the three parts of sin — therefore the Torah obligates man to bring a sacrifice for his sin and to lean his hands upon it corresponding to the deed, and to confess with his mouth corresponding to the speech, and to burn the organs of counsel and thought that agreed in the sin — these being the entrails and the kidneys — corresponding to the thought, so that he may achieve atonement through these three things for the three parts of the sin. And it is fitting that the blood of the sacrifice be dashed upon the altar corresponding to his blood, and when he does all these things he should consider in his heart that he is liable to death for his sin and deserving of punishment by the four death-penalties of the court, which are stoning, burning, slaying, and strangulation. When he takes the animal and casts it and pushes it to the ground, this is like the matter of stoning, as if he admits that he is liable to stoning; and when he slaughters it and presses hard upon its neck at the time of slaughter so as not to prolong it (shehiyah), he has performed slaying and strangulation, as if he admits that he is liable to slaying and strangulation; and when he burns it, it is as if he admits that he is liable to burning." "<b>Similar</b> to this did the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expound regarding the matter of the Binding (Genesis 22:13): ״ויעלהו לעולה תחת בנו״ — 'and he offered him up for a burnt-offering instead of his son.' What is 'instead of his son'? Rather, for every act of service that Abraham performed with the ram, he would pray: May it be Your will that this service be considered as though it were performed upon my son — as though he were slaughtered, as though his blood were dashed, as though he were burned and reduced to ash. And since the sinner deserves that his blood be spilled like the blood of the sacrifice, and that his body be burned like the body of the sacrifice, and the Holy One, blessed be He, accepts his sacrifice from him in its place and as its atonement — this is pure kindness that the Name, may He be exalted, bestowed upon him in His mercy and in the abundance of His lovingkindness: He takes the soul of the animal in place of his soul, and he attains atonement through it. And this reason is sound and satisfying to the heart. And even though the benefit of the sacrifices in this manner is only for the sinner who offers them alone, Scripture still calls the sacrifice ״אשה ריח ניחוח לה׳״ — a fire-offering, a sweet savor unto YHWH — because the will of the Holy One, blessed be He, cleaves to this sinner after he has repented of his sin and offered his sacrifice; and He, exalted be He, commanded him in this, and His will is fulfilled." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: it is written (Psalms 50:9–10), ״אם ארעב לא אומר לך כי לי תבל ומלואה״ — 'If I were hungry, I would not tell you, for Mine is the world and all its fullness' — ״לא אקח מביתך פר ממכלאותיך עתודים כי לי כל חיתו יער בהמות בהררי אלף״ — 'I will not take a bull from your house nor he-goats from your folds, for every beast of the forest is Mine, the cattle upon a thousand mountains.' R. Shimon said: Thirteen attributes of mercy are written of the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said (Exodus 34:6), ״ויעבור ה׳ על פניו״ etc. 'A merciful one delivers his sustenance to the cruel' — you may infer: ״אם ארעב לא אומר לך״. R. Yehudah bar Shimon said: The Holy One, blessed be He, said — Ten species of clean animals I delivered to you: three in your domain and seven not in your domain. Three in your domain: ox, sheep, and goats. Seven not in your domain: gazelle, deer, yachmur, akko, dishon, te'o, and zamer. And from those that are not in your domain I did not burden you to bring from them a sacrifice, but only from those that are in your domain, from those raised in your stall — you may infer: ״אם ארעב לא אומר לך״. R. Yitzchak said: ״את קרבני לחמי״ — 'My offering, My bread' — is there eating and drinking before Him? Learn from the ministering angels, of whom it is written (Psalms 104:4), ״משרתיו אש לוהט״ — 'His ministers are flaming fire'; from what do they sustain themselves? From the radiance of the Shekhinah, as it is said (Proverbs 16:15), ״באור פני מלך חיים״ — 'in the light of the king's face is life.' R. Shimon ben Lakish said: Learn from Moshe Rabbeinu, of blessed memory, for it is written (Exodus 34:28), ״ויהי שם עם ה׳ ארבעים יום וארבעים לילה לחם לא אכל ומים לא שתה״ — 'and he was there with YHWH forty days and forty nights; bread he did not eat and water he did not drink.' And if Moshe, who was on His mission, did not eat bread or drink water, you may infer: ״אם ארעב לא אומר לך״." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): the matter of the sacrifices and their benefit is this — every sinner, whether in thought or in deed, does so from the side of the spirit of impurity that clothes itself in him. And when he wishes to be atoned for, he needs to annihilate and banish from himself that spirit of impurity, and with that he would become purified and atoned. Therefore he must offer the goat or the buck, which belong to the division of the spirit of impurity, from that side; and the fire of the altar burns and annihilates everything together, for the sinner has already burned himself with the fire of the evil impulse, and the fire of the altar comes and prevails over it to annihilate it completely, a decisive annihilation. And this is what they said: 'Fire consumes fire' — the fire of the altar consumes another fire from above; and likewise below — fire consumes fire: the fire of the altar consumes the fire of the evil impulse. Therefore it is never extinguished." "<b>And in the Guide,</b> the Rav, of blessed memory, wrote regarding the reason for sacrifices that they came in the Torah in order to uproot idolatry from the world, because it was the custom of the ancients among the nations — who were worshippers of idols — to build temples and offer sacrifices to the supernal forces, and their thought was that through this their star would be strengthened and exalted and would thereby draw the force of success and increase of good. Thus we find the Egyptians and the Chaldeans, who would worship cattle and sheep. The Egyptians worshipped the form of sheep because they knew its great power on high, through which the world is blessed in the species of sheep, which yields great profit and abundant benefit in fleece, in offspring, and in milk. In honor of its force and its root above, which is the constellation of Aries (מזל טלה), they would not eat it at all, nor would they use it — and the people of Egypt were not shepherds like the other nations; as it is written (Genesis 46:34), ״כי תועבת מצרים כל רעה צאן״ — 'for every shepherd of sheep is an abomination to the Egyptians.' And likewise the Chaldeans worshipped the form of cattle, because cattle are the cause of plowing, which is the sustenance of the world, as it is written (Proverbs 14:4), ״ורב תבואות בכח שור״ — 'and abundant produce by the strength of the ox.' And since they knew in their wisdom the power of cattle and its root above, which is the constellation of the Ox (מזל שור), therefore they practiced a prohibition against slaughtering cattle and would not eat them, in honor of its power, through which the world is blessed in the species of cattle. And even today the people of India are careful not to slaughter cattle. Therefore the Torah commands slaughtering these species to the Holy One, blessed be He — and this is what it says: מן הבקר ומן הצאן — 'from the cattle and from the sheep' — so that it become known that the very thing they considered to be the utmost form of worship is in fact the utmost form of atonement, for by offering them to the Holy One, blessed be He, sins are atoned. For evil beliefs, which are the diseases of the soul, can only be healed by their opposite, just as a disease of the body can only be healed by its opposite. And the entire intention of the Torah is none other than to uproot the remembrance of idolatry from the world and to eradicate it from its roots, to flee from the way of its worshippers, and not to do as their deeds even for the sake of Heaven. And therefore the sacrifices are ״אשה ריח ניחוח לה׳״ — a fire-offering, a sweet savor unto YHWH — for when all agree to offer to the Holy One, blessed be He, and to worship Him shoulder to shoulder, alone and not to idolatry, then the Name of Heaven is unified through this, for there is no unification in the place of idolatry. And for this reason they are His will, exalted be He. This is the way of the Rav, our master Moshe, of blessed memory, and his reasoning. And how sound and sufficient a reason it would be, were it not that we find righteous ones and great ones who offered sacrifices at a time when there was no idolatry in the world. For Adam the First brought a sacrifice, and Hevel also brought one; and Noach and his sons, when they emerged from the ark, offered sacrifices, and their sacrifice was accepted, and Scripture mentions regarding it ריח ניחוח — a sweet savor — as it is said (Genesis 8:21), ״וירח ה׳ את ריח הניחוח״; and there was as yet no Egyptian or Chaldean in all the world." "<b>But</b> the matter of the offerings, which have existed from the days of eternity, is an inward matter and a hidden secret, and by way of unification (yichud), which is a rung and a ladder, I shall rouse you concerning it; I shall lift my mind to it by way of the quadruple path. Everyone who knows it is obligated to conceal it and hide it, and it is not fitting to transmit it except for the glory of its Creator, to solitary individuals, the devout, who tremble at His word. I shall establish the reason of its secret before you with near allusions — near to one who understands, from honeyed sweetnesses, and far from one who does not understand, for the matters are ancient, deep waters." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah:</b> it says that the offering is the unification, and this is the language of קרבן — that is, bringing near the forces and Names of the Holy One, blessed be He, and everyone who brings His Names near to Him unifies. And when it says והקטיר הכהן את הכל המזבחה — 'and the priest shall burn all on the altar' — with the addition of the letter ה, it does not come to obscure but to explain, for it is the final ה of the Name, which receives first and is called 'altar' (מזבח), and from there the thought begins, from level to level, up to the pure thought. And so you find in Chronicles (II Chronicles 29): ויאמר יחזקיהו להעלות העולה להמזבח — it did not say למזבח but להמזבח with the addition of the ה. And likewise (Psalms 20:4): ועולתך ידשנה סלה — with the addition of the ה. עולה אשה — it said עולה because the smoke of the offering rose up to the ה, and the ה reaches it from there and above; and after the smoke is consumed, the flesh turns back and transforms into fire, and this is what it said אשה; and after the fire is annulled, it returns to become spirit (רוח), and that spirit draws near and mingles to be unified in spirit and in fragrance up to the pure thought, and this is what it said ריח — that is, the drawing down of the will and the influx that descends לה׳, that is, to the attribute of mercy; and this is the meaning of ריח ניחוח לה׳ — from the language of (II Kings 2:15) נחה רוח אליהו על אלישע — thus the supernal spirit rests and descends to the attribute of mercy, which is the maintenance of the world. And this is the explanation of the words עולה אשה ריח ניחוח לה׳: for first one must bring up the אשה לה׳, and ריח is the drawing from above to bring down לה׳.\n\nAnd they expounded in Torat Kohanim: עולה אשה ריח ניחוח לה׳ — עולה for its own sake, אשה for its own sake, ריח for its own sake, ניחוח for the sake of נחת רוח, לה׳ for the sake of the One who made the world. And the explanation of this: עולה לשם עולה — for the sake of the tenth level that ascends, and not that there is the end of the intention, but from there the thought begins and from there it comes to holiness. אשה לשם אשים — it is the glory that receives from fear, and both are called אשים, and this is את קרבני לחמי — one might think לחמי, therefore it says לאשי — לאשים אתם נותנים. And likewise in the Midrash on Song of Songs (Song of Songs 1:15): הנך יפה רעיתי — פרנסתי, the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Israel: what you give to אשי is as though you sustain Me. And therefore the word אשה comes with a ה, and it is added like the ה in (Psalms 8:8) צנה ואלפים כלם, for the root of the word is אש, and the reason for the addition is that it did not say אש, so that the word should remain as in its plain sense, and also to allude to the final ה of the Name, and to the fifth one as well, which is called the great fire. ריח לשם ריח — that is, for the sake of the Place from which all the influxes are drawn downward, and from there the influx rests and descends לה׳, which is mercy; and then the supernal and the lowly are blessed — the heavens and the earth and all that is in them — and this is לשם ריח ניחוח אשה לה׳, that is, the descent of the influx and the resting of the spirit from there לה׳, to the attribute of mercy through which the world is maintained. And this is what it said: לה׳ לשם מי שעשה את העולם — as if to say, to the attribute of mercy, without which the world has no maintenance; as they said: 'See that the world is not maintained by the attribute of judgment, and partner with it the attribute of mercy.'" "<b>And behold:</b> each and every attribute would draw sustenance from the offering, and the intention was to draw down and rest the supernal spirit in the attributes and to bring the attributes near to it — and this is the unification, and this is the language of (Numbers 28:2): תשמרו להקריב לי — it did not say לקרב but להקריב, that is, to bring near to Me the forces and the attributes; and therefore it is called קרבן, for it is the bringing near of the forces and the attributes." "<b>And you should be roused</b> by what they said in tractate Yoma, end of the first chapter: 'Even though the fire descends from heaven, it is a commandment to bring from the ordinary.' For when the two fires were intermingled there, it was a sign and token that there is a bond between the ordinary and the supernal might through the offering; and just as these two fires intermingle and are unified one with the other, so the will of the ordinary and that of the supernal are unified one with the other. And likewise the smoke rising from the action of the offerings below was a cause for action and arousal above, in kindling the influx in the sparks of His lights through the channels of the attributes — and this is like the image of two lamps, one above the other: when you light the lower one, through the smoke rising from it, the flame takes hold in the upper one and it lights." "<b>Also,</b> you should be roused by the fact that the עולה offering was male and not female, and the חטאת was female and not male, and the שלמים either male or female. The reason is that the end of the intention in the עולה offering is above the place where male and female are divided — that is, above the attributes in which there are giver and receiver. But the שלמים offering was either male or female, for it belongs to the middle line that brings peace between the attributes and is inclusive of them; and likewise שלמים are the letters of משלים, and I have already mentioned this above. Therefore it is written concerning שלמים: אם זכר אם נקבה. And the חטאת was female and not male, for it belongs to the attribute of 'guarding,' and it is fitting that the offering be female. And the reason the אשם offering was male is that the אשם comes for the liability of כרת, and in order that the sinner be atoned through it and his soul return to the אלהים אשר נתנה — that is, to the בינה, where the root of souls is and where their return lies — therefore it was male, for this place is above the attributes. And this is what our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: 'In the place where penitents stand, the perfectly righteous cannot stand.' And the explanation of the secret of this saying is that the righteous are the foundation of the world, from the 'foundation of the world'; but penitents — their return lifts them higher, for there is their home."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:23:39.174881672Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}